          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do bodies disagree about using a short-acting reliever on its own in asthma?",
          "answer": "**Short answer:** The retrieved guidance rejects reliever-only treatment. The disagreement is narrower than the question suggests.\n\n**Where the guidance agrees.** The asthma guidance (*Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach to controller therapy*) says that treating asthma with a short-acting bronchodilator alone \"is no longer appropriate at any severity.\" It says every adult and adolescent with asthma should receive inhaled-corticosteroid-containing therapy, because reliever-only treatment leaves the underlying inflammation untreated and is associated with severe exacerbations, even in people with infrequent symptoms. It also says heavy reliever use, such as several canisters a year, is a risk marker in its own right.\n\n**Where the guidance differs.** The same document notes a divergence on the preferred reliever in mild asthma:\n- The global strategy guidance describes an inhaled corticosteroid plus formoterol combination as the preferred reliever across steps.\n- Some national panel guidance says a short-acting bronchodilator reliever alongside a regular controller remains appropriate in mild disease.\n\nNote that the second position still includes a regular controller. Both positions reject SABA-only treatment, so the disagreement is about which reliever to pair with a controller, not about SABA alone.\n\n**How strong the evidence is.** The retrieved passages link reliever-only treatment with severe exacerbations and reliever overuse with risk. They do not grade the strength of that evidence, and they do not say how the bodies weighed it. I can't say more than that from these sources.\n\n**Limits of this answer.** The passages name only \"the global respiratory strategy report\" and \"national asthma panel guidance.\" They do not identify the specific national bodies, so I can't say which organization holds which position. A clinician can confirm which guidance applies to you. If you find yourself needing your reliever more often, or you have trouble breathing, call emergency services if breathing is hard at rest, you cannot speak in full sentences, your lips look blue or gray, or your reliever doesn't help.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
